1.text:
In the beam the ionisation produced in the air can be written as an
air kerma

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}= \frac{I}{m_{air}}\;\frac{W}{e}\;,
\qquad \frac{W}{e}\simeq 33.97\;{\rm J\,C^{-1}} 
\end{equation*}

which in the electronic equilibrium limit is the absorbed dose rate
in air at the focus.  For the overlap of the $0.3\times 6\;{\rm mm^{2}}$
focus with the chamber

\begin{equation*}
V_{air}=w_{f}h_{f}L
       =0.30\times10^{-1}{\rm cm}\times0.60\,{\rm cm}\times15.1\,{\rm cm}
       \approx 2.72\times10^{-1}\,{\rm cm^{3}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{air}= \rho_{air}V_{air}
       =1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times0.2718\,{\rm cm^{3}}
       \approx 3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}\; .
\end{equation*}

Hence

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}= \frac{2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}}{3.52\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}}
             \times33.97\;{\rm J\,C^{-1}}
          \approx1.9\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
          \approx 1.9\times10^{2}\;\mu{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

at the focus.  With $\mu_{en}/\rho_{tissue}\simeq\mu_{en}/\rho_{air}$ the
air kerma is to a very good approximation the absorbed dose in
soft tissue at the surface,

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq\dot K_{air}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}} .
\end{equation*}

A surface element of the object is in the $w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$ beam
for the transit time

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{scan}}=
       \frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x/\Delta t}
\end{equation*}

i.e. the quoted

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{\rm (scan\;step\;per\;exposure\;time)}\approx
0.02\;{\rm s}
\end{equation*}

for the nominal 20 ms dwell per pixel.  The dose received in one
passage of the beam is therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot D_{t}\,t_{d}
     \approx1.93\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}{\rm s}
     \approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\;{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per energy, i.e. of the order of a few $\mu{\rm Gy}$ per
single-energy exposure at the surface.  In terms of air kerma

\begin{equation*}
K_{1}\approx\frac{I\,W/e}{m_{air}}\,t_{d}\approx3.9\,\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per 20 ms dwell, $ \approx 1.9\times10^{-4}{\rm Gy/s}$ air kerma rate
at the focus.  For a customary two- to three-energy Laue set this is
$ \sim 8\!-\!12\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ cumulative surface exposure, i.e. a few
$\times10^{-6}\,$Gy to a few $\times10^{-5}\,$Gy, essentially
negligible for diagnostic purposes.  With the usual $W/e=33.97\,{\rm
J/C}$ the often quoted $\approx4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per 0.02 s pixel and
$\approx 0.19\,{\rm mGy\,min^{-1}}$ air kerma rate at the focus is
obtained; the average dose over the $50\,{\rm mm}$ field scanned with
$w_{f}/\Delta x$ overlap is the same $D_{1}$ per surface element.  If
$N_{E}$ energies are summed the cumulative surface dose is $N_{E}D_{1}$,
e.g. $\approx 1.2\times10^{-5}\,{\rm Gy}$ for $N_{E}=3$, essentially the
$ \sim 4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy quoted above.

3.text:
In an air filled ionisation chamber the charge which is collected in a
time $\Delta t$

\begin{equation*}
Q = I\Delta t
\end{equation*}

is the charge of the $Q/e$ ion pairs which have been created in the
active volume.  With the $W$ value of air, $W\approx 33.9\,{\rm eV}$ per
ion pair, the energy which has been put into ionisation in the volume
which the beam intersects in the time $\Delta t$ is

\begin{equation*}
E_{{\rm dep}}= \frac{Q}{e}W=\frac{IW}{e}\Delta t\;,
\end{equation*}

so that the energy dissipation rate in the intersected air mass is

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{\rm air}= \frac{1}{m_{\rm air}}\frac{IW}{e}\; ,
\qquad {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}} .
\end{equation*}

At the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times 6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.8\times10^{-2}\;{\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

is traversed by the photons over the path $L=15.1\,{\rm cm}$ in the
chamber.  The mass of air which is actually irradiated is

\begin{equation*}
m_{air}=A_{f}L\rho_{air}
      =1.8\times10^{-2}\;{\rm cm^{2}}\times15.1\,{\rm cm}
        \times1.293\times10^{-3}\,{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}
      \approx3.5\times10^{-4}\;{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}\;{\rm kg} .
\end{equation*}

Hence with $I=2.0\,{\rm pA}$

\begin{equation*}
\dot E =\frac{IW}{e}=I\frac{W}{e}
       =2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97\,{\rm V}
       \approx6.8\times10^{-11}\;{\rm W}
\end{equation*}

and

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{air}= \frac{\dot E}{m_{air}}
           \approx\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
           \approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in the air at the focus.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq
(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{air}$ the absorbed dose rate in soft tissue at the
surface is the same to a very good approximation,

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq\dot D_{air}\approx2\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\approx0.2\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}} ,
\end{equation*}

i.e.  about one microgray per millisecond of dwell in the 33 keV
beam.  The time a given material point spends in the 0.30 mm wide
focus during the horizontal scan is the dwell time

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}
     =\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x /\Delta t_{e}}
\end{equation*}

which is quoted as

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}\simeq 2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s} .
\end{equation*}

The dose received by a point which is scanned through the focus once
at one energy is therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}\approx\dot D_{t}\,t_{d}\approx
1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s}
\approx3\!-\!4\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}\approx3\!-\!4\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per energy projection, essentially a few microgray at the skin.  With
the usual $W_{air}=33.97\;{\rm eV}$ this is

$D_{1}\approx3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx4\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}$ per
33 keV pass,

of the order $10^{-6}\,$Gy.  In particular

$D_{1}\approx\dot D_{t}t_{d}\approx0.2\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}\times0.02\,{\rm
s}\approx4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$

is the dose deposited in the tissue which is instantaneously in the
$0.30\times6\;{\rm mm^{2}}$ focus.  For an $N_{E}$–energy multiple-
energy acquisition the point dose scales linearly,

$D_{N}\approx N_{E}D_{1}$,

so that e.g. a conventional dual-energy scan gives $\sim 8\;\mu{\rm Gy}$
to the surface, i.e. a completely negligible radiological exposure,
$\ll 1\,$mGy.  This is the familiar $\mu$Gy per projection regime of
third-generation synchrotron computed tomography.  The $\mu_{en}/\rho$
value quoted for air is the one which is used in the
$\Phi E\mu_{en}/\rho$ formulation of the same result, $\dot D=
\Phi E(\mu_{en}/\rho)$, and gives the identical $\approx2\times10^{-4}
{\rm  Gy\,s^{-1}}$ dose rate at the focus.

