pixel per 33 keV pass, i.e. an air kerma rate of $\sim0.19\ {\rm
mGy\,min^{-1}}$ at the focus.

For an $N_{E}$-energy Laue acquisition the point surface dose scales
linearly, $D_{N}\simeq N_{E}D_{1}$.  With the conventional 2-3 energy
set this is $D_{N}\sim8\!-\!12\;\mu{\rm Gy}\sim1\times10^{-5}\ {\rm Gy}$
cumulative at the skin, essentially $\sim10^{-5}\,$Gy, e.g.
$D_{3}\approx3\times3.8\,\mu{\rm Gy}\approx1.1\!-\!1.2\times10^{-5}\ {\rm
Gy}$ for three energies, negligible for diagnostic purposes.  The
quoted $0.328\ {\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$ $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{air}$ is the usual
$\Phi E(\mu_{en}/\rho)$ consistency check and the 50 mm$\times$6 mm
incident beam on the monochromator sets the 50 mm scanned field, the
average dose over the field being the same $D_{1}$ per surface element.

public_text.3:
In electronic equilibrium the air kerma rate in the overlap of the
focused beam with the air filled chamber is the ionisation power
per unit air mass

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air} = \frac{1}{m_{air}}\;\frac{IW}{e}
           =\frac{I}{m_{air}}\;\frac{W}{e}\;\;,
\qquad \frac{W}{e}\simeq33.97\;{\rm J\,C^{-1}} ,
\end{equation*}

which with $\mu_{en}/\rho_{t}\simeq\mu_{en}/\rho_{air}$ is the
absorbed dose rate in soft tissue at the surface.

For the $w_{f}\times h_{f}$ focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}
      =0.03\,{\rm cm}\times0.60\,{\rm cm}=1.8\times10^{-2}{\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

the mass of air which is actually irradiated in the $L=15.1\,{\rm cm}$
chamber is

\begin{equation*}
m_{air}=A_{f}L\rho_{air}
      =1.8\times10^{-2}\times15.1\times1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g}
      \simeq3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg} ,
\end{equation*}

so that

\begin{equation*}
\dot E =\frac{IW}{e}=2.0\times10^{-12}\,{\rm A}\times33.97\,{\rm V}
       \simeq6.8\times10^{-11}{\rm W}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}=\dot D_{air}= \frac{\dot E}{m_{air}}
           \simeq\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
           \simeq1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

at the $0.30\times6.0\,{\rm mm^{2}}$ focus, i.e. $\approx0.19\,{\rm
mGy\,min^{-1}}$ air kerma rate at 33 keV.  With
$(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\approx(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{air}$

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq\dot K_{air}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in soft tissue at the surface.

A surface element spends a transit time in the $w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$
focal spot

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}\simeq0.020\;{\rm s}
\end{equation*}

in the horizontal scan.  The absorbed dose received in one passage
at one Laue energy is therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot D_{t}\,t_{d}
     \simeq1.93\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}{\rm s}
     \simeq3.7\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\approx3\!-\!4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per 20 ms dwell, $\approx4\,\mu{\rm
Gy}$ per 33 keV projection at the skin.  In air kerma

$K_{1}\approx(IW/e)/m_{air}\;t_{d}\approx3.9\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per
energy.

For an $N_{E}$-energy Laue acquisition the point dose scales
linearly

\begin{equation*}
D_{N}\simeq N_{E}D_{1}\approx N_{E}\times4\,\mu{\rm Gy},
\end{equation*}

so that a conventional two-energy set gives $\sim7\!-\!8\,\mu{\rm Gy}$
and a three-energy set $\sim11\!-\!12\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ cumulative
surface absorbed dose, $D_{3}\simeq1.2\times10^{-5}\,{\rm Gy}$, i.e.
$\sim10^{-5}\,{\rm Gy}$ total at the skin.  This is the familiar
$\mu$Gy per projection regime of third generation synchrotron
imaging and is essentially radiologically negligible.  The often
quoted per-energy value is $D_{1}\approx4\times10^{-6}\,{\rm Gy}$,
$\approx0.19\,{\rm mGy\,min^{-1}}$ air kerma rate at the focus, the
cumulative value being $N_{E}$ times that.
